[{"record_id":"rec_000144","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Early Diagnosis and Personalized Treatment of Alzheimer’s Disease","abstract":"Artificial intelligence (AI) has become a disruptive technology in the medical care industry, with potential solutions to early diagnosis and customized treatment of Alzheimer’s disease (AD), a progressive neurodegenerative disease and the most prevalent cause of dementia globally. Conventional diagnostic techniques, such as cognitive, neuroimaging and biomarker techniques, are usually limited in the ability to detect disease at its most susceptible stage when treatment interventions are most effective. The recent developments in AI, specifically machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing, and predictive analytics, have greatly enhanced the capacity to detect subtle patterns of diseases influenced by complex multimodal data. The AI analysis of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers, blood biomarkers, genomic data, speech patterns, and digital health data has shown incredible prospects of improving the diagnostic accuracy and allowing early detection of the Alzheimer disease. In addition, AI can facilitate precision medicine by combining both patient-specific clinical, genetic, environmental, and lifestyle data to create personalized treatment approaches and maximize therapeutic decision-making. Innovative uses of AI, including treatment planning, disease progression forecasting, digital therapeutics, wearables, and remote monitoring are all part of increasingly proactive and patient-centered healthcare provision. Explainable AI is also enhancing the transparency and clinical acceptance of AI-based decision-support systems. Notwithstanding its promising prospects, issues concerning data privacy, algorithm bias, interpretability, regulatory approval, and clinical validation should be considered as crucial factors to the wider implementation. This review provides an overview of the existing uses of artificial intelligence in early diagnosis and personalized treatment of Alzheimer disease, emphasizing recent technological developments, clinical uses, current limitations and opportunities. Further adoption of AI in neurology can transform the care of dementia using predictive, preventive, and tailored healthcare practices that enhance patient outcomes and quality of life","doi":"10.37591/ijbs.v03i02.247576","year":"2026","venue":"International Journal of Brain Sciences","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000144","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI applications in Alzheimer’s disease, not a primary model paper. The abstract mentions NLP and other AI methods, but not a specific candidate model with a clear text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“This review provides an overview of the existing uses of artificial intelligence in early diagnosis and personalized treatment of Alzheimer disease.”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000144","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a review of AI applications in Alzheimer’s disease, not a primary model description. It discusses methods and uses rather than a specific generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“This review provides an overview of the existing uses of artificial intelligence”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000145","title":"From uncertainty to clarity: The evolution of Nuclear Medicine towards precision theranostics and AI-driven digital twins.","abstract":"Giammarile F(1), Paez D, Brink A, Lobato EE, Mikhail M, Knoll P.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Hellenic journal of nuclear medicine","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000145","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"Title suggests an overview of nuclear medicine and AI-driven digital twins, and the record provides no abstract content describing a primary model. This is not a candidate primary text-bio model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“From uncertainty to clarity: The evolution of Nuclear Medicine towards precision theranostics and AI-driven digital twins.”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000145","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record provides only a title and author line, so this appears to be a broad overview rather than a model paper. No generative architecture or foundation-model evidence is stated.","evidence_snippet":"Title mentions \"the evolution of Nuclear Medicine\" and \"AI-driven digital twins\"; abstract is only author names."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000148","title":"Abstract B028: Repurposing adult clinical trials for rare cancer immune target discovery using the CURE AI foundation model","abstract":"Conventional research begins with an observation that is relevant to human health followed by years of testing in a laboratory environment. This type of 'forward translation' research is the basis for most therapeutic development but fails in over 90% of therapeutic candidates that enter early phase trials. We propose that 'big data' approaches have not solved this issue because the architecture to connect and appropriately analyze large multimodal datasets has not been adequately developed. We will demonstrate how, once data is appropriately harmonized, even relatively modest patient numbers can inform valuable insights into rare cancers by utilizing insights from more common cancer types. To enable this, our engineering team developed an integrated platform called CURE AI that (1) integrates previously siloed cross-disease clinical and multi-omic patient-level data, (2) learns new fundamental biological principles from the relationships that exist within and across diseases, (3) finetunes a base foundation model to become an expert within specific areas such as predicting organ toxicity, predicting therapeutic benefit, or developing disease-site expertise, and (4) applies these insights through 'reverse translation' for indication expansion, target discovery, biomarker selection, toxicity prediction, and combination therapy selection. We will present several examples of how we utilize CURE AI to identify complex biomarkers of adult cancer treatment predictions, which we have proven already to be predictive across different common cancer types in adults, to identify new rare cancer patient populations of high predicted benefit to immunotherapies. We will discuss how we are currently identifying new immunotherapy targets and biomarkers by harmonizing rare cancer data with adult cancer datasets and how this guides our therapeutic pipeline strategy in Merkel cell carcinoma, mesothelioma, and pediatric cancers.\n \n \n Vitalay Fomin, Amit Weiss, Radesh P. Nattamai Malli, Tal Shor, Daniel Khankin, Ofir Landau, Gregory Koushnir, Tzvi Lederer, Gal Netzer, Neil Pfister. Repurposing adult clinical trials for rare cancer immune target discovery using the CURE AI foundation model [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the AACR Special Conference in Cancer Research: Breaking Barriers in the Fight against Rare Cancers; 2026 Jul 18-20; Philadelphia, PA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2026;86(14_Suppl):Abstract nr B028.","doi":"10.1158/1538-7445.rareca26-b028","year":"2026","venue":"Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000148","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a foundation model built on cross-disease clinical and multi-omic patient-level data, but it does not clearly describe a text or language component. That makes it out of scope for a generative text-bio model screen.","evidence_snippet":"“integrates previously siloed cross-disease clinical and multi-omic patient-level data” and “finetunes a base foundation model”."},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000148","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This abstract describes a platform that fine-tunes a base foundation model for downstream oncology use cases, which makes it an application wrapper. The generative mechanism itself is not explicit.","evidence_snippet":"\"finetunes a base foundation model to become an expert within specific areas\" and applies it to rare cancer discovery."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000153","title":"Frozen but Not Always Accessible: A Representation Analysis of Genomic Language Models","abstract":"Genomic foundation models are increasingly reused as frozen feature extractors for downstream sequence prediction, offering a compute-efficient alternative to full fine-tuning. However, it remains unclear when biological information encoded by these models is accessible without task-specific adaptation. We present a representation-accessibility analysis of frozen genomic language models across regulatory, epigenetic, promoter, splice-site, and variant-effect prediction tasks. We evaluate DNABERT-2, Nucleotide Transformer, HyenaDNA, GENERATOR-v2, and Omni-DNA under unified frozen-probing protocols, while separating diagnostic readout analyses from validation-selected checks. Our results reveal a consistent task-dependent pattern: frozen probes recover 95-100 % of fine-tuned performance on promoter tasks, but average splice-site recovery drops to 60-88 %. Frozen embeddings are also competitive on broad Genomic Benchmark tasks such as coding-region and species-discrimination classification, but show larger gaps on some regulatory and OCR tasks. Layer-wise probing, in-silico mutagenesis, variant-effect prediction, and embedding geometry show that local biological signal is partially present in frozen representations, but is not always accessible through final pooled embeddings.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000153","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract studies genomic language models via frozen probing and representation analysis, but it does not present a new model. It clearly involves biological sequence models with language-model-style representations.","evidence_snippet":"“genomic foundation models are increasingly reused as frozen feature extractors” and “representation-accessibility analysis of frozen genomic language models”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000153","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract analyzes frozen genomic language models as feature extractors, but it does not describe a new generative architecture. It does, however, clearly frame them as genomic foundation models reused across tasks.","evidence_snippet":"“Genomic foundation models are increasingly reused as frozen feature extractors for downstream sequence prediction”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000156","title":"Abstract A041: Glyco-Immune Checkpoint Activation in Inflammatory Breast Cancer Defines a Targetable Immune Evasion Program","abstract":"IBC is a highly aggressive breast cancer subtype defined by rapid progression, dermal lymphatic invasion, collective tumor emboli formation, and poor response to standard therapies. Aberrant O-glycosylation, including pathologic accumulation of truncated Tn and sTn antigens, has been implicated in immune escape across solid tumors, yet its relationship to the mechanobiological and disseminative programs unique to IBC remains poorly understood. We hypothesized that IBC harbors a coordinated transcriptional state linking aberrant glycosylation, macrophage-mediated immune suppression, and checkpoint activation with therapeutic relevance.\n \n \n \n Transcriptomic analyses were conducted in two independent cohorts: a discovery cohort (GSE45581; 20 IBC, 20 non-IBC, 5 normal) and a validation cohort (GSE22597; 25 IBC, 57 non-IBC). Curated gene sets representing glycosylation remodeling, truncated O-glycan biology, MGL-macrophage immune suppression, mechanotransduction, emboli-associated dissemination, immune exhaustion, and checkpoint activation were used to construct composite GlycoCheckpoint scores. Scores were evaluated across differential expression analyses, correlation networks, and multivariable linear regression. Analytical pipeline development was assisted by large language model-based coding tools.\n \n \n \n In the discovery cohort, glycosylation-associated programs were significantly enriched across tissue types (Kruskal-Wallis p=0.012), driven predominantly by tumor-versus-normal differences; IBC and non-IBC tumors showed comparable activation (p=0.51). In the validation cohort, GlycoCheckpoint activation correlated with cytokine-mediated immune programs (rho=0.30, p=0.006) and checkpoint signaling (rho=0.24, p=0.029). In multivariable models adjusting for emboli-associated dissemination, mechanotransduction, and IBC status, GlycoCheckpoint activation remained independently associated with cytokine-mediated immune suppression (β=0.383, p=4.38×10-5), checkpoint signaling (β=0.305, p=0.003), and exhaustion programs (β=0.290, p=0.007). Driver-gene analyses identified nominally significant differential expression of mucin glycoproteins (MUC1, MUC4), glycosyltransferases (ST3GAL4, GALNT6), and MGL-macrophage axis components (CLEC10A, CD163) in IBC versus non-IBC tumors.\n \n \n \n These data support a putative glyco-immune checkpoint state in IBC in which aberrant glycosylation, involving mucin glycoproteins, Tn/sTn-associated glycosyltransferases, and MGL-macrophage signaling, is independently coupled to cytokine-mediated immune suppression, checkpoint activation, and T-cell exhaustion. This coordinated architecture suggests that targeting aberrant O-glycosylation and MGL-macrophage immune suppression may sensitize IBC tumors to PD-1/PD-L1 checkpoint blockade. These findings nominate glyco-immune reprogramming as a potentially targetable immune evasion program in inflammatory breast cancer. Generative AI was used to assist in editing the text of this abstract.\n \n \n \n Ecem Kalemoglu, Adil Bahadir. Glyco-Immune Checkpoint Activation in Inflammatory Breast Cancer Defines a Targetable Immune Evasion Program [abstract]. In: Proceedings of AACR Drug Discovery and Development (AACR D3) Conference; 2026 Jul 21-24; Boston, MA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Clin Cancer Res 2026;32(14_Suppl):Abstract nr A041.","doi":"10.1158/1557-3265.d32026-a041","year":"2026","venue":"Clinical Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000156","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a transcriptomic biology study, and the only AI mention is that generative AI helped edit the abstract. It does not describe a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“Generative AI was used to assist in editing the text of this abstract”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000156","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This abstract reports a transcriptomic analysis with composite scores and regression, not a generative model. The only AI mention is that coding tools assisted editing the abstract.","evidence_snippet":"“Transcriptomic analyses were conducted” and “Generative AI was used to assist in editing the text of this abstract”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000164","title":"VESTIGE: A Knowledge-Guided Masking Strategy for Corruption-Aware Fine-Tuning of Genomic Transformers, Validated on Ancient DNA Reconstruction","abstract":"Standard masked-language-model fine-tuning applies a uniform masking probability across every token position, assuming reconstruction difficulty is position-agnostic. When the degradation process is characterised and concentrated at predictable positions, this assumption fails: at peak damage sites the model can underperform a frequency-matched random predictor. We introduce VESTIGE, a parameter-free, drop-in replacement for the standard MLM collator that aligns the masking distribution with an empirically measured per-position corruption profile. We apply it to ancient DNA (aDNA) reconstruction, where cytosine deamination produces a position-dependent C-to-T / G-to-A gradient quantified per-position by mapDamage2. Rescaling so the mean C/G masking rate equals 15% - identical to standard MLM - isolates spatial redistribution as the sole variable, with model, data, seed, and hyperparameters held fixed across both DNABERT-2 runs on a mammoth CDS corpus (two specimens, seven genes). Across six terminal-zone widths and 626 paired windows, VESTIGE leads standard MLM at every width (Delta = +4.18 to +10.35 pp, all p<10^-8), cuts validation cross-entropy by 13% (3.274 vs. 3.757), and yields ESMFold reconstructions with TM-score>0.95 across all six reconstructions (three genes) even under damage amplified 10-30x beyond authentic PMD rates. A 1D CNN biosecurity classifier returns AUC = 0.935 and clears 98.2% of reconstructed windows, the 1.76% remainder attributable to reference-genome features, not reconstruction artefacts. The principle is domain-agnostic: any measurable position- or context-specific corruption profile - FFPE, bisulfite, metagenomic, or nanopore - substitutes directly for the PMD array, making VESTIGE a knowledge-guided training routine for intelligent systems operating on degraded or noisy sequence inputs.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000164","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes masked-language-model fine-tuning of genomic transformers on ancient DNA reconstruction, which is a clear sequence-language modeling setup. The bridge between token modeling and biological sequence reconstruction is substantive.","evidence_snippet":"standard masked-language-model fine-tuning ... apply it to ancient DNA reconstruction ... DNABERT-2 runs"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000164","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a drop-in masking/collator strategy for fine-tuning an existing genomic transformer, not a new generative architecture. Its focus is corruption-aware training for ancient DNA reconstruction.","evidence_snippet":"\"drop-in replacement for the standard MLM collator\"; \"fine-tuning\"; \"DNABERT-2\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]

